From a26b01afa3041b831cd49a8d52e5d50b215618c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DeltaTor <deltator@example.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 18:25:59 +0330
Subject: [PATCH 37/38] conflux: measure probe throughput over active time, not
 idle time

The measured-throughput estimate divided a window's delivered bytes by the
whole wall-clock window. But a window is only rolled when a new stream is
placed, so `elapsed` includes every second in which nobody used the set. A set
that moved its bytes in a two-second burst and was then idle for a minute was
therefore reported as roughly 1/30th of its real speed, and a set that merely
had no traffic looked slow enough to decay.

Record the monotonic timestamps of the first and last payload byte counted in
the current window (one cheap call from circuit_read_valid_data(), which
already updates the set's byte counter) and divide by that active span
instead. A single delivered cell counts as a 1 ms span, and the wall-clock
window remains the upper bound because the two clocks are independent.

The roll lines now print both numbers (`elapsed` and `active`) so the
correction is visible, and the estimate itself only uses `active`.
